<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Computing work</title>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Imagine that a cable, 50 meters long and having mass 150
        kilograms, is hanging from the top of a building.  We
        will compute the amount of work needed to lift the entire
        cable to the top of the building.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Assuming that the cable is uniform, how much mass is in
          every meter of the cable?  That is, what is the density
          of the cable in kilograms per meter?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="16">
        <p>
          Density is mass divided by length so <m>\rho=150/50=3</m>
          kilograms per meter.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let's set up a coordinate system: <m>y</m> will measure
          the vertical distance upwards from the bottom of the
          cable.
        </p>
        <image width="8%">
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                     label="16-cable-1">
            <xi:include href="prefigure/16-cable.xml"/>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
        <p>
          Imagine that we take a super thin slice of width
          <m>dy</m> at location <m>y</m>.  Sketch the slice above
          and use the density to determine the mass <m>dm</m> of
          this slice.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="16">
        <p>
          Mass equals density times length so <m>dm=3~dy</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Imagine that the segment is very thin.  How far does
          this segment need to be lifted when <m>y = 10</m>?
          <m>y = 20</m>?  <m>y = 30</m>?  for a general value
          of <m>y</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="16">
        <p>
          If <m>y=10</m>, the slice needs to be raised by 40 meters.
          In general, the distance is <m>50-y</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          How much work <m>dW</m> is performed in lifting just
          this segment to the top of the building?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="16">
        <p>
          <m>dW = (50-y)g~dm = g(50-y)3~dy</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now that you know the amount of work required to lift
          each segment to the top of the building, determine the
          total work required to raise the entire cable to the top
          of the building.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="16">
        <p>
          <md>
            W = \int dW = 3g\int_0^{50}(50-y)~dy = 36750
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
          Here is a similar problem.  Consider a cone whose height
          and base radius are each 2 meters.  You may imagine
          creating this cone by taking the line <m>y=x</m> between
          <m>0\leq x\leq 2</m> and spinning it about the
          <m>y</m>-axis.  Now imagine that this cone is filled with
          water and we want to know how much work is required to
          pump the water up to the top of the cone.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.7in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Imagine we take a thin horizontal cylindrical slice of
          the cone at position <m>y</m> and of height <m>dy</m>.
          What is the volume <m>dV</m> of this slice?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="16">
        <p>
          The cross sectional radius of the cone is <m>y</m> so
          <m>dV = \pi y^2~dy</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.7in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The density of water is <m>1000</m> kg/m<m>^3</m>.
          What is the mass <m>dm</m> of this slice?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="16">
        <p>
          <m>dm=1000~dV = 1000\pi y^2~dy</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.7in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          How far does this slice need to be lifted to raise it to
          the top of the cone?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="16">
        <p>
          The distance is <m>2-y</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.7in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          How much work <m>dW</m> is required to raise this slice
          to the top of the cone?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="16">
        <p>
          <m>dW = g(2-y)~dm = 1000g\pi(2-y)y^2~dy</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          How much work is required to pump all the water out of
          the cone?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="16">
        <p>
          <md>
            W = \int dW = 1000g\pi \int_0^2 (2-y)y^2~dy = 41050
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose now that we have a spherical water tank of radius
        3 meters.  You may imagine forming this tank by spinning
        the right half of a circle <m>x^2+y^2=9</m> about the
        <m>y</m>-axis.  Suppose that the tank is filled with
        water to a depth of one meter and that the density of the
        water is <m>1000-y</m> kilograms per cubic meter.
      </p>
      <image width="25%">
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                   label="16-sphere">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/16-sphere.xml"/>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the volume of water in the tank.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="16">
        <p>
          In the given coordinate system, we have
          <m>x=\sqrt{9-y^2}</m> so <m>dV = \pi(9-y^2)~dy</m>.  Then
          <md>
            V = \int dV = \int_{-3}^{-2} \pi(9-y^2)~dy = 8.38
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the center of mass of water in the tank.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="16">
        <p>
          First we need to find the mass where <m>dm=(1000-y)~dV =
          (1000-y)\pi(9-y^2)~dy</m>.  Then
          <md>
            M=\int dm = \int_{-3}^{-2}(1000-y)\pi(9-y^2)~dy = 
            8397.2
          </md>.
          Then
          <md>
            \int y~dm = \int_{-3}^{-2}(1000-y)\pi(9-y^2)y~dy = -19681
          </md>
          so that
          <md>
            \ybar = \frac{-19681}{8397.2} = -2.34
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the amount of work required to pump all of the
          water to the top of the tank.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="16">
        <p>
          <m>dW = (3-y)g~dm</m> so
          <md>
            W = \int_{-3}^{-2} (1000-y)g(3-y)(9-y^2)~dy = 44873
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
